 <P STYLE="margin-top:0pt; margin-bottom:0pt; font-size:10pt; font-family:Times New Roman" ALIGN="center"><B><A NAME="rom741934_17"></A>DILUTION </B></P>
<P STYLE="margin-top:12pt; margin-bottom:0pt; text-indent:4%; font-size:10pt; font-family:Times New Roman">Purchasers of our common stock in this offering will experience immediate dilution of the net tangible book value of our common stock to the
extent of the difference between the public offering price per share of common stock and the net tangible book value per share of our common stock at the time of purchasing shares in this offering. Our historical net tangible book value as of
September 30, 2014 was approximately $371 million, which represents $7.85 per share of common stock. Net tangible book value per share is determined by dividing our total tangible assets less our total liabilities by the number of shares of common
stock outstanding. </P> <P STYLE="margin-top:12pt; margin-bottom:0pt; text-indent:4%; font-size:10pt; font-family:Times New Roman">Assuming that (1)&nbsp;we raise the maximum offering amount of $2.7 billion, (2)&nbsp;all shares sold after September
30, 2014 are Class&nbsp;A shares and (3)&nbsp;all such Class&nbsp;A shares are sold for $10.42 per share (excluding selling commissions), which represents the NAV per Class&nbsp;A share as of September 30, 2014, after deducting offering expenses,
our pro forma net tangible book value as of September 30, 2014 would have been approximately $3.1 billion, or $9.96 per Class&nbsp;A share. Assuming that (1)&nbsp;we raise the maximum offering amount of $2.7 billion, (2)&nbsp;all shares sold after
September 30, 2014 are Class M shares and (3)&nbsp;all such Class M shares are sold for $10.44 per share, which represents the NAV per Class M share as of September 30, 2014, after deducting offering expenses, our pro forma net tangible book value
as of September 30, 2014 would have been approximately $3.1 billion, or $9.98 per Class M share. Assuming that (1) we raise the maximum offering amount of $2.7 billion, (2) all shares sold after September 30, 2014 are Class A-I shares and (3) all
such Class A-I shares are sold for $10.44 per share, which represents the NAV per Class A-I share as of September 30, 2014, after deducting offering expenses, our pro forma net tangible book value as of September 30, 2014 would have been
approximately $3.1 billion, or $9.98 per Class A-I share. Assuming that (1)&nbsp;we raise the maximum offering amount of $2.7 billion, (2) all shares sold after September 30, 2014 are Class M-I shares and (3) all such Class M-I shares are sold for
$10.44 per share, which represents the NAV per Class M-I share as of September 30, 2014, after deducting offering expenses, our pro forma net tangible book value as of September&nbsp;30, 2014 would have been approximately $3.1 billion, or $9.98 per
Class M-I share. </P> <P STYLE="margin-top:12pt; margin-bottom:0pt; text-indent:4%; font-size:10pt; font-family:Times New Roman">As a result, assuming all shares sold after September 30, 2014 are Class&nbsp;A shares, there would be an immediate
increase in net tangible book value to our existing stockholders of $2.11 per share, and an immediate dilution in pro forma net tangible book value to new purchasers of Class&nbsp;A shares of $0.46 per share. Assuming all shares sold after September
30, 2014 are Class M shares, there would be an immediate increase in net tangible book value to our existing stockholders of $2.13 per share, and an immediate dilution in pro forma net tangible book value to new purchasers of Class M shares of $0.46
per share. Assuming all shares sold after September 30, 2014 are Class A-I shares, there would be an immediate increase in net tangible book value to our existing stockholders of $2.13 per share, and an immediate dilution in pro forma net tangible
book value to new purchasers of Class A-I shares of $0.46 per share. The following table illustrates this per share dilution related to the primary offering: Assuming all shares sold after September 30, 2014 are Class M-I shares, there would be an
immediate increase in net tangible book value to our existing stockholders of $2.13 per share, and an immediate dilution in pro forma net tangible book value to new purchasers of Class M-I shares of $0.46 per share. </P>
